  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting Stability in Iraq through the Yazidi Female Survivors Law</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Iraq has been affected by cycles of conflict and violence since 1970s, often marked by human rights violations and a lack of effective response. The genocide and crimes against humanity committed by ISIL against Yazidis and other religious minorities in Iraq (Shabaks, Turkmens and Christians) have had profound impact on these communities and on Iraq more broadly. Thousands of civilians have been killed or are still missing, while approximately 5,000 women and children have been abducted and exposed to crimes of sexual violence or forced recruitment. The Government of Iraq (GoI) passed the Yazidi Survivors Law in 2021, to address the legacy of ISIL violations against minorities in Iraq &#x2013; comprehensive reparations for survivors of sexual violence and other crimes, including financial allocation and benefits for survivors. Due to lack of technical knowledge, gaps in the implementation persist and threaten effective implementation. IOM addresses this through i) supports General Directorate for Survivors Affairs (GDSA) to strengthen capacity to operationalize the Law and provide reparations</narrative>
  </description>
